# SYNTAX TEST "text.html.tiddlywiki5" "Raw markup carries no verdict"

# Nothing inside raw markup answers to wikitext, so no rule reaching for a construct reaches
# past the boundary of a fence, an inline tick or a typed block. An angle bracket carries no
# verdict anywhere — TiddlyWiki builds a node for every one — and these hold the boundary
# itself, which a rule that gains a verdict later must still respect.

Inline `a < b` code.
#          ^ - invalid.illegal.bad-angle-bracket.html.tiddlywiki5
#          ^ markup.inline.raw.single.tiddlywiki5

```
a < b in a bare fence
# ^ - invalid.illegal.bad-angle-bracket.html.tiddlywiki5
```

```notalanguage
x < y
# ^ - invalid.illegal.bad-angle-bracket.html.tiddlywiki5
```

$$$text/plain
x < y
# ^ - invalid.illegal.bad-angle-bracket.html.tiddlywiki5
$$$

# A stray bracket in prose carries no verdict either — TiddlyWiki builds a text node for it.
A stray < in prose.
#       ^ - invalid.illegal.bad-angle-bracket.html.tiddlywiki5
#       ^ text.html.tiddlywiki5 meta.paragraph.tiddlywiki5

# An xml fence names its own grammar.
```xml
<a/>
```
